examples.question_text:
Three empty containers are filled at the same constant volume rate. Their vertical cross-sections are shown. Sketch a water-height–time graph for each container. Label the axes.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw (-0.5,2)--(-0.5,0)--(0.5,0)--(0.5,2);
\draw (2,2)--(2.6,0)--(3,0)--(3.6,2);
\draw (4.8,2)--(4.2,0)--(5.8,0)--(5.2,2);
\path (0,-0.4) node {(a)} (2.8,-0.4) node {(b)} (5,-0.4) node {(c)};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
For (a), the cross-section stays constant, so the height rises at a constant rate: draw a straight rising line.
For (b), the container widens upwards, so the height rises quickly at first, then more slowly: draw a rising curve that gets flatter.
For (c), the container narrows upwards, so the height rises slowly at first, then more quickly: draw a rising curve that gets steeper.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{rateblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\begin{scope}
\draw[->] (0,0)--(2.5,0);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,2.3);
\path (1.25,-0.4) node {Time} (-0.45,1.15) node[rotate=90] {Height} (1.2,2.65) node {(a)};
\draw[thick,rateblue] (0,0)--(2.1,1.9);
\end{scope}
\begin{scope}[xshift=3.7cm]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(2.5,0);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,2.3);
\path (1.25,-0.4) node {Time} (-0.45,1.15) node[rotate=90] {Height} (1.2,2.65) node {(b)};
\draw[thick,rateblue] (0,0) .. controls (0.25,1.1) and (1.1,1.65) .. (2.1,1.9);
\end{scope}
\begin{scope}[xshift=7.4cm]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(2.5,0);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,2.3);
\path (1.25,-0.4) node {Time} (-0.45,1.15) node[rotate=90] {Height} (1.2,2.65) node {(c)};
\draw[thick,rateblue] (0,0) .. controls (1,0.25) and (1.85,0.8) .. (2.1,1.9);
\end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The container is narrow near the bottom, widest in the middle and narrow again near the top. The height therefore rises quickly, then more slowly, then quickly again. Draw a rising curve that becomes flatter and then steeper.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{rateblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[->] (0,0)--(4,0);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,3.3);
\path (2,-0.4) node {Time} (-0.45,1.65) node[rotate=90] {Height};
\draw[thick,rateblue] (0,0) .. controls (0,0.8) and (0.8,1.25) .. (1.8,1.5) .. controls (2.8,1.75) and (3.6,2.2) .. (3.6,3);
\path (0.65,0.5) node[right] {Fast rise} (1.8,1.15) node[below] {Slowest rise} (2.7,2.7) node[left] {Fast rise};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.question_text:
An empty container has a narrow cylindrical lower section and an upper section that gradually widens. Its vertical cross-section is shown. Water enters at a constant volume rate. Sketch the water-depth–time graph through both sections.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw (-1.2,3)--(-0.35,1.1)--(-0.35,0)--(0.35,0)--(0.35,1.1)--(1.2,3);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
In the cylindrical section, the depth rises at a constant rate: start with a steep straight line. In the widening section, the depth keeps rising but its rate decreases: continue with a curve that gets flatter. Join the pieces smoothly because the width is continuous at the join.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{rateblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[->] (0,0)--(4.4,0);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,3.2);
\path (2.2,-0.4) node {Time} (-0.45,1.6) node[rotate=90] {Depth};
\draw[thick,rateblue] (0,0)--(0.5,1.1) .. controls (0.8,1.76) and (1.8,2.45) .. (3.8,2.9);
\draw[dashed] (0.5,0)--(0.5,1.1);
\path (0.5,-0.85) node {Upper section begins};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Initially the water surface is wide, so removing water lowers the depth slowly. As the surface becomes narrower, the same volume removed causes a larger depth drop. Draw a falling curve that becomes steeper and reaches zero when the container is empty.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{rateblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[->] (0,0)--(4.2,0);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,3.2);
\path (2.1,-0.4) node {Time} (-0.45,1.6) node[rotate=90] {Depth};
\draw[thick,rateblue,domain=0:1,samples=121,variable=\u] plot ({3.6*(1-(1-\u)*(1-\u)*(1-\u))},{2.8*(1-\u)});
\path (3.6,-0.85) node {Empty};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

(a) Start with a horizontal speed segment. Draw a smooth dip that stays above zero, then return to the original horizontal level.
(b) Start at zero height, rise to a rounded maximum, then fall back to zero. The ball is momentarily at rest at the top; there is no horizontal interval there.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{rateblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\begin{scope}
\draw[->] (0,0)--(4.3,0);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,2.7);
\path (2.15,-0.4) node {Time} (-0.45,1.35) node[rotate=90] {Speed} (2,3.05) node {(a)};
\draw[thick,rateblue] (0,2.1)--(0.7,2.1) .. controls (1.2,2.1) and (1.2,0.55) .. (2,0.55) .. controls (2.8,0.55) and (2.8,2.1) .. (3.3,2.1)--(3.9,2.1);
\end{scope}
\begin{scope}[xshift=5.6cm]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(4.3,0);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,2.7);
\path (2.15,-0.4) node {Time} (-0.45,1.35) node[rotate=90] {Height} (2,3.05) node {(b)};
\draw[thick,rateblue,domain=0:3.8,samples=61,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{2.2*(1-((\x-1.9)/1.9)^2)});
\end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

(a) Start at the origin. Draw a rising curve that is steep initially and gets flatter: increasing at a decreasing rate.
(b) Start above zero. Draw a rising curve that gets steeper: increasing at an increasing rate.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{rateblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\begin{scope}
\draw[->] (0,0)--(3.7,0);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,2.8);
\path (1.85,-0.4) node {Time} (-0.45,1.4) node[rotate=90] {Price} (1.8,3.15) node {(a)};
\draw[thick,rateblue,domain=0:3.2,samples=61,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{2.5*(1-exp(-0.9*\x))});
\end{scope}
\begin{scope}[xshift=5cm]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(3.7,0);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,2.8);
\path (1.85,-0.4) node {Time} (-0.45,1.4) node[rotate=90] {Pollution level} (1.8,3.15) node {(b)};
\draw[thick,rateblue,domain=0:3.2,samples=61,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{0.3+0.22*\x+0.16*(\x)^2});
\end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Start at $85^\circ\mathrm{C}$. Draw a steep falling curve that gets flatter as it approaches $23^\circ\mathrm{C}$. The temperature decreases at a decreasing rate and stays above room temperature during the time shown.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{rateblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[->] (0,0)--(4.8,0);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,3.5);
\path (2.4,-0.4) node {Time} (-1,1.75) node[rotate=90] {Temperature};
\draw[dashed] (0,0.65)--(4.4,0.65);
\draw[thick,rateblue,domain=0:4.3,samples=81,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{0.65+2.45*exp(-0.95*\x)});
\path (-0.12,3.1) node[left] {$85^\circ\mathrm{C}$} (2.6,0.65) node[below] {Room temperature $23^\circ\mathrm{C}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

(a) Draw a straight rising segment, a horizontal resting segment, then a shallower straight falling segment ending at zero. The return takes longer because the walking speed is lower.
(b) Start at $18\mathrm{\,cm}$. Draw a straight falling segment to zero, then continue horizontally at zero.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{rateblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\begin{scope}
\draw[->] (0,0)--(4.8,0);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,2.8);
\path (2.4,-0.4) node {Time} (-0.45,1.4) node[rotate=90] {Distance from home} (2.3,3.15) node {(a)};
\draw[thick,rateblue] (0,0)--(1.2,2.2)--(2,2.2)--(4.4,0);
\path (1.6,2.2) node[above] {Rest};
\end{scope}
\begin{scope}[xshift=6.3cm]
\draw[->] (0,0)--(4.8,0);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,2.8);
\path (2.4,-0.4) node {Time} (-0.85,1.4) node[rotate=90] {Height (cm)} (2.3,3.15) node {(b)};
\draw[thick,rateblue] (0,2.2)--(2.8,0)--(4.4,0);
\path (0.12,2.2) node[above right] {$18$} (2.8,-0.85) node {Used up};
\end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Start above zero with a horizontal segment. Continue with a rising curve that gets steeper, then flatter, and finally joins a horizontal segment smoothly.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{rateblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[->] (0,0)--(5.8,0);
\draw[->] (0,0)--(0,3.4);
\path (2.9,-0.4) node {Time} (-0.85,1.7) node[rotate=90] {Population};
\draw[thick,rateblue] (0,0.6)--(0.8,0.6) .. controls (1.4,0.6) and (1.8,1.15) .. (2.3,1.7) .. controls (2.8,2.25) and (3.2,2.8) .. (3.8,2.8)--(5.3,2.8);
\path (-0.12,0.6) node[left] {$1200$} (0.4,0.95) node[above] {Steady} (1.3,2.05) node {Faster growth} (3.8,1.65) node {Slower growth} (4.55,3.05) node {Steady};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Both steady stages are horizontal. During growth, the population keeps increasing even when the curve becomes flatter.
